  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Quiet weather conditions are anticipated \nthe rest of the week.\n\nDry and sunny weather conditions are expected locally through \nmidweek. High pressure will build directly overhead today, and \nthen move just offshore Wed. Today will be the coolest day of \nthe week, with highs in the 60s for most. Tonight will be \nanother cool night, but with the high a bit further east, \nlocations east of the Blue Ridge will have the greatest chance \nto decouple. Lows tonight are forecast to be in the upper 30s to\nmid 40s. Temps warm a bit on Wed in S'ly flow, with high temps \nforecast to be in the 70s for most.\n\nAn embedded shortwave trough passes by to the north on Thu which\nushers in the next cold front. Like the early week system, this\nfrontal passage will also be a dry one. Thus, the peak in \nwarmth during the period is likely on Thu with highs reaching \nthe upper 70s to low 80s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Remaining dry through the first half of the\nweekend before rain chances return by Sunday.\n\nThe weather during the second half of the upcoming weekend into \nearly next week will be dictated at least in part to an area of low \npressure currently in the Bay of Campeche.\n\nSynoptically, a large longwave trough will be lifting out of the \nNortheast to start the weekend. Meanwhile, the aforementioned low \npressure area will drift generally northward to the east/southeast \nside of an upper-level low over Texas. Ridging pivoting across the \nUpper Midwest will attempt to merge with ridging building over the \nsouthwest Atlantic from near Bermuda to the Bahamas. The main \nuncertainty appears to stem from the depth and forward speed of low \npressure as it approaches the Gulf Coast, which will determine how \nit navigates around the areas of ridging.\n\nAt this juncture, several scenarios appear possible:\n\n(1) Quick-moving low pressure moves onshore of the Gulf Coast, and \nthreads the needle between high pressure over the Southwest Atlantic \nand the Upper Midwest, allowing it to move further north toward the \nOH Valley, central Appalachians and Mid-Atlantic, delivering notably \nhigher rain potential and amounts locally.\n\n(2) A very quick-moving low pressure system is forced to make an \neastward turn sooner across the Southeast prior to the western \nextent of SW Atlantic ridging exiting the coast. This would keep a \nmajority of moisture and rain chances to the south, though some \nmoisture could still be drawn northward along a passing front.\n\n(3) A slow-moving low pressure system is forced to make that \neastward turn sooner, blocked in this scenario by the second area of \nhigh pressure in the Upper Midwest building in before it can trek \ntoo far to the north. This would keep nearly all precipitation and \nmoisture associated with the low suppressed to the south.\n\n(4) A very slow-moving area of low pressure would not only get \ncaught beneath ridging building over the Upper Midwest, but could \nthen remain cutoff from the large scale steering pattern. This would \nleave low pressure meandering somewhere well to our south for a \ntime, before an uncertain exit beyond the current forecast period.\n\nAt the moment, deterministic and ensemble medium range guidance \nexhibits very large spread, falling into (but not necessarily \nfavoring) any one of the four scenarios listed above.\n\nOtherwise, temps should generally trend cooler or stay \nrelatively steady, but will depend highly on the extent of cloud\ncover or any precip (or lack thereof).",
